lyst it's  oftentimes used for model systems our  group has studied it a lot and many  people have followed that up with  with more experiments because this is a  really promising photo catalyst but it  suffers from poor carrier mobility so  these excited charge carriers could  perform photo catalytically but instead  they oftentimes self-trap which leads to  recombination and a quenching of any  photocatalytic activity  and this happens on this time scale of a  few hundred femtoseconds so being able  to probe where these electron and holes  are moving within your system is really  critical for understanding this process  better  um and what we find in our study was  that  there were predictions that indicated  that the kinetics of polaron formation  and hopping are unique to the surface  versus the bulk and they converge to  bulk behavior within a few atomic layers  so here's a contour plot of um the  excited hematite state excited with 400  nanometer light  and we see that um you know before time  zero there's no signal and then as you  excite your sample you have this  negative feature which  is there very early and shifts to  blue shifts to a higher energy and then  we have this positive feature which  grows in as well  so the model that was developed  is that in the excited state so we  excite with our optical light we have  this initial charge transfer state  um  the excited electrons can couple to  optical phonon modes in the hematite  sample and this results in an ultra fast  lattice distortion  and this is followed by polaron  formation  within several hundred femtoseconds  but if we take a look at the time scales  so  specifically the polaron state we can  notice that in the bulk  which is the black line this happens on  a time scale of 90 femtoseconds but at  the surface with xcdra which we which we  can probe we see that this is um  quite slower about three times slower at  250 femtoseconds  so the next example that i wanted to  highlight is the resolution between  electron and holes in metal oxide which  i have described just briefly before but  in the molecular image of looking at a  metal oxide structure we notice that  with a pump excitation from a valence to  conduction band we'll have a hole in our  oxygen valence band and a new electron  in our metallic conduction band what  this means is that a metallic emits  transition now we've lost some  some absorption potential because we  filled an empty state here whereas in  the oxygen l edge we've now gained  some xv intensity because we have a new  excitation pathway where our hole is so  by  understanding this molecular picture we  can actually  start to realize what the and predict  what the xv spectrum might look like we  would have positive intensity where we  have new absorption pathways and a  negative intensity where an electron is  now blocking  an absorption pathway  so this translates to  our spectra here on the right where we  have different metal edges nickel oxide  cobalt oxide and iron oxide again all of  these will appear at different energies  because of that element specific core  structure but in each case we have  positive and negative features where we  have new or removed absorption pathways  and in these three examples we also see  our oxygen l edge we have a positive  intensity here because we have that new  hole in our oxygen  ledge transition now in the 2p state the  only time we don't see our oxygen led is  in this iron disulfide where of course  we have no oxygen but what we do see is  that the iron positive and negative  features line up perfectly with the iron  oxide positive and negative features  because that iron center is not changing  the oxygen l edge you'll notice is  shifting a little bit this is  a direct relation to how those oxygen 2p  bonding states are covalently  interacting with a metal  metal center and this can be shifted  because of the covalency of that metal  and then at the bottom of this we have  this search paper  which is uh  going to directly show us a very similar  trend where we have um xcv  differentiating character carriers even  in single element semiconductors so this  case is for germanium where we have this  band diagram which shows what we're  exciting  basically a visible or near ir pump will  excite electrons into the conduction  band and leave behind a hole in the  valence band and the xuv beam can probe  both that hole and that electron by  showing us an increased intensity at the  hole and a decreased intensity  absorption where the electron is now  occupying that state so we have at the  whole an increased intensity and at the  electron we have a negative intensity  here  so these karyodynamics and  nanocrystalline  germanium or  other metallic systems can reveal  separate  dynamics that that importantly decay  together as those charges recombine so  that's really essential to assigning  those carriers  okay and then the final example that i  want to highlight  is  dynamics of spin states resolved by xuv  spectroscopy so here we studied some um  kind of complicated cobalt iron oxide so  we have two different metal centers  and this cobalt ferrite is a fairy  magnetic inverse spinel ferrite so the  iron three plus centers occupy all of  the tetrahedral and half of the  octahedral sites and then cobalt two  plus occupies half of the octahedral  sites  so here we found that with um  with xuv ra spectroscopy we were  actually able to  model and simulate the high spin versus  the low spin excited states  and for the sake of time i won't get  into a lot of the details of that  fitting but  basically we found that with this system  we could model that we have a high spin  initial state where the cobalt is in the  high spin and then we have a low spin  final state  which grows in as a function of time so  we're actually inducing a spin flip or a  spin crossover event on the time scale  of about 400 femtoseconds so this is  very similar to previous reports in  prussian blue analog systems  but we're able to resolve this uh ground  state excitation into a high spin state  and then decay into a low spin excited  state and this is really important  because we're understanding charge  carrier dynamics with spin sensitivity  which means we can  model  um  and sort of optimize materials that need  this sort of spin control  okay so some future directions for the  field that i'll highlight briefly  first there's a lot of work happening  right now to extend the capabilities of  hhg produced xuv light into the water  window which is between 280 to about 535  ev this will allow us to access carbon  nitrogen and oxygen k edges which are  really critical for most systems we find  a lot of carbon nitrogen and oxygen  present in  interesting metallic catalytic etc  systems  and we would also be able to study this  in liquid environments which is  something we're limited to in the baker  group we only can probe  solid solid systems and we could do  gases right now although solids are  usually more interesting so here's  just a spectrum where you can see that  the photon energy produced is greatly  expanded  um the second  future direction i'll highlight is xuv  magnetic circular dichroism where we  have the generation of circularly  polarized coherent xuv light um so we  produce circularly polarized rather than  linearly polarized xv light and then we  can use this light at the sample which  is in some magnetic field  to understand spin events at interfaces  and at surfaces so this is a figure from  a different reference which i'm  highlighting here they have a a  different set up so the setups are not  very uniform right now but basically  we're starting to look at xmcd signal  rather than x right mcd or um you know  just traditional magnetic circular  dichroism we can extend this into the  xev window and we're currently actively  working on this very diligently so  hopefully we'll have some papers to show  um you know in the short term and then  the final  application or future direction that um  i'll highlight is liquid sheet studies  so rather than liquid jet studies which  have been around for some time now um in  in the cylindrical jet options we have a  strong absorption at the the liquid  edges of the jet we have photon  scattering happen and refraction due to  that boundary between the liquid and the  vacuum and we also have a mismatch  between the jet diameter and the focus  size of the xev beam so  liquid jets have not really been  a promising  option for xuv studies but  by flattening those jets into these  liquid sheets which look something like  this  we can actually have tunable very thin  sheets that are able to  sort of propagate enough xv light that  we can study liquid samples or we can  study um sort of a solute in or sorry  it's a  solute inside of some kind of liquid  okay and then the last thing that i'll  highlight is just that there are some  ongoing technological advances also  underway to increase the brightness of  some tabletop light sources so the  national extreme ultra fast science  facility or nexus is one effort  so nexus is an nsf  sponsored uh mid-scale user facilities  so something between like a synchrotron  and a lab-based instrument this will be  open access to users  but it is being hosted at ohio state and  the director is dr baker mypi so at the  heart of this facility is a kilowatt  class ultra fast laser which provides  two orders of magnitude increase in the  xcv pulse repetition rate compared to  traditional hhg sources like in my lab  um  and so we have uh these specs for our  our laser that's coming in and we can  drive out of second and femtosecond xuv  and soft x-ray generation with this  laser  this xuv light will  will provide um  light for the these experimental end  stations so we have x-ray absorption or  x-ray reflection spectroscopy which is  what i've highlighted today  but we'll also have xcv light for angle  resolved photoelectron spectroscopy or  rpes  element specific scanning tunneling  microscopy or stm and out of second  science with uh laser induced electron  diffraction or lied  so  this isn't the only facility there's  also one in europe which is uh the  extreme light infrastructure auto second  light pulse source or eli alps and  facilities like these are really opening  the door to a variety of non-linear xv  measurements as well as ultra fast  in-situ measurements  that are currently limited by just a  lack of sufficient xv photon flux at at  larger scale facilities  so again just to highlight what nexus  specifically will do i won't get into  details here but  just a few examples of the different end  stations as well as the time resolutions  we expect here so going down to the auto  second resolution  the xuv specs on target are  um you know can be quite small and can  can have quite a broad uh  energy excitation energy window so 10 to  70 v is traditionally what we do that's  what will be provided for our pez but  for the xas and xrs at nexus we'll have  an expanded window from 15 up to 300 ev  so just to summarize with the nexus  facility why are we focused on these  higher repetition rates what's this  general benefit going to provide to us  well we're actually enabling a quantum  leap and sample throughput so in our  current state of the art high harmonic  generation and a tie staff laser system  which we have in our lab with a one  kilohertz repetition rate you know dr  baker writes okay the required sample  averaging is greater than 10 hours per  sample in reality we usually average on  a sample for a transient experiment up  to a few weeks of of experimentation but  at the nexus facility where we have 100  kilohertz now suddenly this is going to  take us a 10 hour sample would take us  less than 10 minutes so this is going to  really affect how much xuv science can  be done by  researchers across the country and  really across the globe in general at  this mid-scale facility you know it's  not going to be as big as the  synchrotron or an x-ray free electron  laser but we're really going to enable a  high throughput of different science and  different different projects that can  come through so lastly i'll just put put  out our little advertisement that we  have a user workshop coming up on july  25th and 26th  and because andor is really critical to  our development we're using an andor  camera for  the xas edge station um i'm not sure  about i work with xas so i'm not sure  about the other end stations but i'm  sure andor  would be well represented at this user  workshop and you can hear about a lot  more exciting and  you know upcoming potential experiments  that could happen at nexus as its  development comes to an end in the next  year or two  so with that i would like to  wrap up my talk and open the door to any  questions that you may have here i'm  listing my contact information as well  as dr baker's if you have questions and  here is again the citation for  the  the recent publication that i have  sort of modeled much of this information  on that goes into more detail there  and thank you for your time and i'm  happy to  thank you emily for your informative  presentation  we will now start the live q a portion  of the webinar  if you have a question you'd like to ask  please do so now just click on the ask a  question box located on the far left of  your screen we'll answer as many of your  questions as we have time for  so let's get started our first question  is  what kind of ionization medium is used  for the high harmonic generation process  okay this is a great question and we  actually had another question that came  in on a similar topic so  in the baker group we typically stick  with something like helium gas  or argon gas  um these provide us with pretty high  flux in the region that we're interested  in which again is about 30 to 70 ev but  really any um noble gas that has an  ionization potential within the xv  window  could be usable and depending on the gas  you choose you can tune a little bit  much energy  region you  get the most xcv flux within  um and just to address the second half  of the other question that came in about  the spot size so with our setup um and  our focal length from our toroidal  mirror we usually have  um  the  the xcv beam that's about one millimeter  in width and then because of that  grazing angle it's actually stretched so  the height is  usually like point one millimeters  thank you  next question  how thin does a transmission sample need  to be to allow xuv flux through  okay this is a good question as well so  yeah one of the complications of doing a  sample in transmission mode would be  that the xcv is going to attenuate  through  the sample as it passes through the bulk  of it  so the the thickness is typically on the  order of a few hundred nanometers  although you can again  try to tune this depending on the  material properties itself and the  attenuation coefficient as well as the  total flux of fcv you have going into  the sample  great thank you  next question  in example one  why does the negative feature electron  blue shift but the positive feature hole  not shift  okay this is a good question and this  kind of highlights why we can really  focus on  differentiating between the electrons  and holes throughout the system so  in the case of that example where we had  excited hematite the  um the polarone formation was due to the  stretching of the iron oxygen bond  and as that bond shifts or adds up on  stretches sorry the  the shift is actually due to the  electron becoming more localized on the  oxygen itself from that metal to ligand  charge transfer so we see a shift in the  electron feature but not in the whole  feature which is unaffected in this  lattice distortion  thank you  next question  what is the timeline for the nexus  project to be completed  okay this is a great question as well so  the initial nsf proposal was kind of  frameworked on this five-year plan and  we're hoping to spend  a few years in design and purchasing an  acquisition of everything and then  another couple years building and  testing all of the systems  because of the past few years we've had  a lot of shipping delays and  manufacturing delays that have slowed  this timeline down but i think we are on  on track to  have our first light this summer and  we'll begin  uh building all of the beam lines and  sort of finalizing the the end stations  themselves so that we can test and start  to accept user proposals within  hopefully the next uh two years but  i think this this type of question will  be answered in more detail at the user  workshop if anyone's interested in  joining in july  thank you it looks like we have time for  one more question  in a transmission experiment how does  the absorption through the bulk of the  sample affects the collected data  so yeah so in the transmission samples  as your activity beam goes completely  through the sample you will have an  effect of the absorption of the bulk of  your sample and because the bulk can  have  different properties and behaviors in  the surface your output data will have  a lot of information on the bulk and  depending on what you classify as the  surface which in our group would be the  first few nanometers if you go through  100 nanometers of sample you really  can't say much about what you're what  you're detecting at the surface you'll  have too much interference in the bulk  and i think this is one of the biggest  benefits of suv reflection  experimentation where you can just  isolate what's happening at the surface  layer  great and we do have time for one last  question  is the xuv technique suitable for  nanoparticle samples  okay that's a good question so  the the main limitation on the activity  technique is that everything is  happening under vacuum so our group has  started to  dabble in some more complicated and  sensitive samples  the  vacuum conditions as well as the probe  energy and intensity will  have to kind of be investigated on an  individual basis to determine the  stability of the sample but in general  nano particular samples are possible and  have been studied in a few other xv  groups so yes it is possible but a  little bit more challenging  great thank you again emily for your  time today and your important research  we would also like to thank labyrus and  our sponsor andor for underwriting  today's educational webcast  before we go i'd like to thank the  audience for joining us today and for  their interesting questions  questions we did not have time for today  and those submitted during the on-demand  period will be addressed by the speaker  via the contact information you provided  at the time of registration  can be viewed  and  that email with your colleagues who may  have missed today's live event  until next time goodbye  you

